<p>A standard microcycle in this framework looks like this:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Monday:</strong> Rest. Carbohydrates kept low to moderate. High protein.</li>
  <li><strong>Tuesday:</strong> Short intervals (Zone 4/5). Fully fueled. Carbohydrates prioritized at breakfast and pre-workout.</li>
  <li><strong>Wednesday:</strong> 45-60 minutes low Zone 2. Executed fasted upon waking. High fat oxidation.</li>
  <li><strong>Thursday:</strong> 45-60 minutes low Zone 2. Executed fasted.</li>
  <li><strong>Friday:</strong> Threshold work (Zone 4). Fully fueled.</li>
  <li><strong>Saturday:</strong> Long run (Zone 2). Partially fueled. You may start fasted, but you begin ingesting 30-60g of carbohydrates per hour after the first 60 minutes to protect muscle tissue as the duration extends.</li>
  <li><strong>Sunday:</strong> Complete rest or 30 minutes active recovery (Zone 1).</li>
</ul>

<p>This structure protects your threshold. The two hard days receive the glycogen they need. The three easy days force the fat oxidation adaptation. The 2g/kg of daily protein repairs the damage.</p>


<h2>When the zones feel stale</h2>

<p>As you lose weight and your aerobic efficiency improves, your internal metrics will decouple from your external output. A pace that used to put your heart rate at 145 bpm will suddenly only require 132 bpm.</p>

<p>When your perceived exertion drops at prescribed paces, and your heart rate refuses to climb into the correct zone, your anchors are stale. Do not artificially force the pace higher on easy days to chase a heart rate number. That leads straight back to Zone X.</p>

<p>Instead, this is the trigger to re-test. We mandate a new 30-minute threshold field test every 2 to 3 months for consistent athletes. You recalculate your threshold pace and your lactate threshold heart rate, input them into the zone calculator, and reset the seven zones. The progression is mathematically validated, not guessed.</p>


<h2>Choosing the right plan</h2>

<p>A well-designed plan orchestrates these variables for you. It schedules the FAT-MAX runs, dictates the interval paces, and integrates the strength training so that the two disciplines do not ruin each other. A heavy leg day should not fall the day before your hardest interval session.</p>

<div class="datanote">
  <p><strong>Note on training metrics:</strong> Every weight loss running plan in the Triaperformance catalogue uses <strong>pace</strong> as the primary metric, not heart rate. When operating in a caloric deficit, heart rate fluctuates wildly based on hydration, fatigue, and caffeine. Prescribing by pace ensures you hit the exact mechanical and metabolic targets required, without being misled by the lag of an optical wrist sensor.</p>
</div>

